// A `?` whose enclosing function declares its error type as a **type parameter** defers: `E` is not
// a concrete type, so there is nothing yet to demand a `From<string>` of, and the judgement belongs
// to the instantiation site. (E0057 is what fires when both sides ARE concrete and disagree — see
// `results/try_from_missing.noe`.)
//
// The deferral used to be spelled "a `Type::Named` head whose name is in the in-scope
// type-parameter table". The moment a parameter got a lattice variant of its own it stopped
// matching that arm and fell into the `_ => true` catch-all beneath it, and `outer` below reported
// E0057 against `E`. A rule whose trigger can rot out from under it while every existing test still
// passes is exactly what this case guards.
// expect: stdout "1"
// expect: exit 0
fn inner(): Result<int, string> {
    return Ok(1);
}

// The propagated error is concrete, the declared one is a parameter.
fn outer<E>(): Result<int, E> {
    v = inner()?;
    return Ok(v);
}

// Both sides are the SAME parameter — the case that kept working, because equality caught it first.
fn same<E>(): Result<int, E> {
    v = outer::<E>()?;
    return Ok(v);
}

echo match same::<string>() { Ok(n) => "${n}", Err(e) => "err ${e}" };
